WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.847
This briefing comes from Book 16 of
the Federal Modernization Workbook,

2
00:00:04.847 --> 00:00:06.925
People: Training and Education.

3
00:00:06.925 --> 00:00:09.487
It's written for C I Os and CISOs,

4
00:00:09.487 --> 00:00:14.200
workforce and training leads,
I S S Os, and help desk leads.

5
00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:17.947
I'll take four questions: why
people, not tools, close the gap;

6
00:00:17.947 --> 00:00:21.537
what the program teaches;
who owns workforce readiness;

7
00:00:21.537 --> 00:00:25.362
and how to run the curriculum
and measure whether it worked.

8
00:00:25.362 --> 00:00:29.463
The short version: every book before
this one changes what someone does.

9
00:00:29.463 --> 00:00:33.121
Tools enforce the new rules,
but people run the estate,

10
00:00:33.121 --> 00:00:36.300
and a program that trains last pays twice.

11
00:00:37.050 --> 00:00:42.138
A Conditional Access policy can require
phishing-resistant sign-in for every user.

12
00:00:42.138 --> 00:00:45.325
It can't decide what happens
when a caller tells the help

13
00:00:45.325 --> 00:00:48.651
desk their security key is lost
and they need to sign in now.

14
00:00:48.651 --> 00:00:51.943
An entitlement review can
list everyone with access,

15
00:00:51.943 --> 00:00:54.338
but it can't make the owner read the list.

16
00:00:54.338 --> 00:00:57.379
A drift check can find a
server that left its baseline,

17
00:00:57.379 --> 00:01:01.112
but it can't say whether the
exception behind it was ever approved.

18
00:01:01.112 --> 00:01:03.895
The program's position
is that this technology

19
00:01:03.895 --> 00:01:06.463
moves risk from configuration to judgment.

20
00:01:06.463 --> 00:01:10.475
What's left is a smaller number of
human decisions that carry more weight.

21
00:01:10.475 --> 00:01:15.385
And almost every book splits a job into
deciding and doing, and moves some

22
00:01:15.385 --> 00:01:20.213
decisions to people outside I T, such
as HR staff and application owners.

23
00:01:20.213 --> 00:01:23.742
A person who doesn't know the new
rule falls back on the old habit,

24
00:01:23.742 --> 00:01:27.510
which is exactly what the
modernization was meant to remove.

25
00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:32.760
Many programs train at the end:
build, migrate, then train.

26
00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:36.322
The program's observation is
that this is paid for twice.

27
00:01:36.322 --> 00:01:38.348
The first payment comes at cutover.

28
00:01:38.348 --> 00:01:41.647
Users who weren't taught the
new sign-in call the help desk.

29
00:01:41.647 --> 00:01:46.206
A help desk that wasn't taught the new
verification either refuses legitimate

30
00:01:46.206 --> 00:01:50.623
callers or resets accounts without the
checks meant to stop impersonation.

31
00:01:50.623 --> 00:01:54.353
Administrators ask for standing
roles until things settle,

32
00:01:54.353 --> 00:01:57.561
and exceptions granted
under pressure tend to stay.

33
00:01:57.561 --> 00:01:59.672
The second payment comes afterward.

34
00:01:59.672 --> 00:02:03.572
The workarounds have to be found
and removed, people retrained,

35
00:02:03.572 --> 00:02:06.797
and habits unlearned, which
is slower than learning.

36
00:02:06.797 --> 00:02:10.197
Federal rules already put
training before the change.

37
00:02:10.197 --> 00:02:13.406
OPM's rule requires awareness
material before access,

38
00:02:13.406 --> 00:02:18.397
and training whenever the system environment
or procedures change significantly.

39
00:02:18.397 --> 00:02:22.936
NIST's controls ask for training
when system changes require it,

40
00:02:22.936 --> 00:02:28.673
and OMB Circular A-130 requires role-based
training before access is authorized.

41
00:02:28.673 --> 00:02:33.620
So training last also puts the agency
behind requirements it already has.

42
00:02:34.370 --> 00:02:38.157
FISMA is a staffing requirement
as much as a course requirement:

43
00:02:38.157 --> 00:02:42.087
the agency head must have trained
personnel sufficient to comply,

44
00:02:42.087 --> 00:02:46.445
and the C I O trains and oversees people
with significant security duties.

45
00:02:46.445 --> 00:02:51.857
OPM's rule names five audiences, from
every user to I T operations staff.

46
00:02:51.857 --> 00:02:55.508
Circular A-130 has the
chief human capital officer

47
00:02:55.508 --> 00:02:59.158
and the C I O set competency
requirements together.

48
00:02:59.158 --> 00:03:04.489
And the Federal Cybersecurity Workforce
Assessment Act requires every agency to

49
00:03:04.489 --> 00:03:08.893
identify its cyber positions and
code each one to the NICE Framework,

50
00:03:08.893 --> 00:03:11.520
NIST's vocabulary of forty-two work roles.

51
00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:13.407
The codes haven't been reliable.

52
00:03:13.407 --> 00:03:18.105
In 2019, GAO found that 22 of
24 agencies had given a non-I T

53
00:03:18.105 --> 00:03:23.620
code to about nineteen percent of
their I T management positions.

54
00:03:23.620 --> 00:03:28.045
So the program treats codes as one
roster input, not settled fact.

55
00:03:28.045 --> 00:03:30.913
Just as important is what
the rules don't require:

56
00:03:30.913 --> 00:03:34.785
no civilian certification mandate,
and the Defense Department's

57
00:03:34.785 --> 00:03:38.720
eighty-one forty program doesn't
apply to civilian agencies.

58
00:03:39.470 --> 00:03:41.145
Now what the program teaches.

59
00:03:41.145 --> 00:03:43.408
It separates education from training.

60
00:03:43.408 --> 00:03:47.979
Education builds the mental model,
for everyone, before any tool,

61
00:03:47.979 --> 00:03:53.069
and it teaches six ideas in order,
because each depends on the one before.

62
00:03:53.069 --> 00:03:56.070
Governance is a system
that runs continuously.

63
00:03:56.070 --> 00:03:58.132
Identity is the starting point.

64
00:03:58.132 --> 00:04:00.969
One map replaces many inventories.

65
00:04:00.969 --> 00:04:04.182
Every derived record names
its source and version.

66
00:04:04.182 --> 00:04:09.294
Modernization is a lifecycle under
change control, not a project that ends.

67
00:04:09.294 --> 00:04:11.458
And drift is the feedback signal.

68
00:04:11.458 --> 00:04:15.930
Someone who holds those ideas understands
why the help desk now asks for

69
00:04:15.930 --> 00:04:19.209
verification, why a role is
activated instead of held,

70
00:04:19.209 --> 00:04:21.370
and why a screenshot isn't evidence.

71
00:04:21.370 --> 00:04:26.011
Orientation comes as a ninety-minute
walkthrough, a self-serve written path,

72
00:04:26.011 --> 00:04:29.695
and a forty-five-minute briefing
for auditors and assessors.

73
00:04:29.695 --> 00:04:33.455
Training comes after, by role,
in four tracks: compliance,

74
00:04:33.455 --> 00:04:38.278
for people who evidence controls;
implementation, for people who build;

75
00:04:38.278 --> 00:04:43.540
vendor training and labs; and end-user
training for everyone who signs in.

76
00:04:44.290 --> 00:04:49.415
The compliance track is for the people
who read evidence rather than build it:

77
00:04:49.415 --> 00:04:54.140
I S S Os, compliance staff, control
assessors and authorizing officials.

78
00:04:54.140 --> 00:04:57.215
It teaches one skill
above the others, tracing.

79
00:04:57.215 --> 00:05:01.706
Given a control, name the mechanism
that closes it, the book that built it,

80
00:05:01.706 --> 00:05:06.502
the record it emits and where that record
sits in the authorization package.

81
00:05:06.502 --> 00:05:10.789
Given a record, work back to its
control and say whether it's current.

82
00:05:10.789 --> 00:05:13.721
The orientation module carries four ideas:

83
00:05:13.721 --> 00:05:18.118
the Risk Management Framework's
seven steps; who owes which proof,

84
00:05:18.118 --> 00:05:22.271
with only the authorizing
official accepting residual risk;

85
00:05:22.271 --> 00:05:25.609
Moderate and FedRAMP's Class
C as two facts, not one;

86
00:05:25.609 --> 00:05:31.065
and FedRAMP's forty-six key security
indicators binding providers, not agencies.

87
00:05:31.065 --> 00:05:36.290
Eight modules then follow the program's
evidence areas, from identity to training.

88
00:05:36.290 --> 00:05:40.137
Each one teaches a discipline:
a policy screenshot can't show

89
00:05:40.137 --> 00:05:44.215
that a sign-in was phishing-resistant;
only the sign-in record can.

90
00:05:44.215 --> 00:05:48.570
The capstone is a continuous-monitoring
narrative for one system.

91
00:05:49.320 --> 00:05:54.114
The implementation track teaches the
same estate from the engineer's chair,

92
00:05:54.114 --> 00:05:58.832
in ten build modules from D N S and
address management to day-two operations.

93
00:05:58.832 --> 00:06:03.043
The order is a real dependency
chain: address before you identify,

94
00:06:03.043 --> 00:06:07.566
identify before you shape transport,
transport before you place data,

95
00:06:07.566 --> 00:06:10.607
and certificates before
the network enforces.

96
00:06:10.607 --> 00:06:13.424
Devices, the cloud platform and security

97
00:06:13.424 --> 00:06:16.820
operations run in parallel
once identity exists.

98
00:06:16.820 --> 00:06:20.570
Day two comes last, because it
runs what everything else built.

99
00:06:20.570 --> 00:06:22.758
Every module ends the same way.

100
00:06:22.758 --> 00:06:27.220
Done means it works and its evidence has
landed where an assessor can read it.

101
00:06:27.220 --> 00:06:32.528
Every lab teaches three habits: observe
before you enforce, with report-only,

102
00:06:32.528 --> 00:06:37.077
monitor and shadow modes; keep a
way back in, with emergency access

103
00:06:37.077 --> 00:06:41.458
accounts created first; and export
the record, not a screenshot.

104
00:06:41.458 --> 00:06:45.308
Certifications are examples,
not proof, and they change.

105
00:06:45.308 --> 00:06:53.708
Microsoft retired AZ-500 on August
31, 2026, and SC-500 replaced it.

106
00:06:53.708 --> 00:06:58.160
So the program re-checks its
certification table at every gate.

107
00:06:58.910 --> 00:07:01.685
Federal guidance asks
for more than completion.

108
00:07:01.685 --> 00:07:06.201
A-130 asks agencies to test users'
knowledge, and NIST's learning-program

109
00:07:06.201 --> 00:07:10.410
guide recommends practical
assessments that mimic the work role.

110
00:07:10.410 --> 00:07:13.442
So the program certifies
only what a person can show,

111
00:07:13.442 --> 00:07:15.910
with an artifact someone else could check.

112
00:07:15.910 --> 00:07:19.260
Every capstone is scored
on one four-level scale.

113
00:07:19.260 --> 00:07:20.535
Zero is missing.

114
00:07:20.535 --> 00:07:24.660
One names the right books but gets
the controls or the evidence wrong.

115
00:07:24.660 --> 00:07:28.360
Two traces the chain from book
to control to evidence source.

116
00:07:28.360 --> 00:07:31.573
Three is assessor-ready:
a real, dated result,

117
00:07:31.573 --> 00:07:35.410
with an honest statement of
what isn't evaluated yet.

118
00:07:35.410 --> 00:07:39.539
The compliance capstone passes
with every area at two or better,

119
00:07:39.539 --> 00:07:43.435
at least three at three, and all
four whole-submission checks.

120
00:07:43.435 --> 00:07:46.602
An after-action report with
no findings scores zero,

121
00:07:46.602 --> 00:07:50.873
and so does a training percentage
with no per-person records behind it.

122
00:07:50.873 --> 00:07:55.948
Two credentials follow: practitioner for
builders, assessor for compliance staff.

123
00:07:55.948 --> 00:07:58.072
Both expire after twelve months.

124
00:07:58.072 --> 00:08:02.520
An industry certification can waive
a module, but never a capstone.

125
00:08:03.270 --> 00:08:05.158
Vendors teach their products,

126
00:08:05.158 --> 00:08:08.632
and the book names them as
examples, never endorsements.

127
00:08:08.632 --> 00:08:12.208
A vendor course earns a place
only if it passes four tests:

128
00:08:12.208 --> 00:08:16.089
it maps to a book procedure; it's
current; the agency can keep its

129
00:08:16.089 --> 00:08:20.882
completion record; and the agency knows
how the vendor handles learner data.

130
00:08:20.882 --> 00:08:25.157
Exam records tied to a work account
are lost when the person leaves,

131
00:08:25.157 --> 00:08:27.333
so the agency keeps its own record.

132
00:08:27.333 --> 00:08:30.920
Lab environments are harder to
get than most programs expect.

133
00:08:30.920 --> 00:08:33.632
Microsoft Learn's free sandboxes are gone.

134
00:08:33.632 --> 00:08:38.355
The developer sandbox has eligibility
limits and isn't offered in any

135
00:08:38.355 --> 00:08:42.508
government cloud, and there's no
free sandbox in G C C High at all.

136
00:08:42.508 --> 00:08:46.457
So the program office arranges lab
tenants, and before each gate the

137
00:08:46.457 --> 00:08:50.758
team rehearses once in the agency's
own non-production government tenant.

138
00:08:50.758 --> 00:08:56.067
Every lab follows the same rules: never
production; emergency access first;

139
00:08:56.067 --> 00:09:00.692
observe before enforcing; no sync
or federation with production;

140
00:09:00.692 --> 00:09:04.310
synthetic data only; and
tear it down at the end.

141
00:09:05.060 --> 00:09:07.160
Every user will sign in differently.

142
00:09:07.160 --> 00:09:09.385
Federal policy sets the target.

143
00:09:09.385 --> 00:09:15.017
OMB's zero trust memo and NIST's authentication
guideline require phishing-resistant

144
00:09:15.017 --> 00:09:18.772
sign-in for staff, contractors
and partners, and passwords,

145
00:09:18.772 --> 00:09:21.823
one-time codes and push
approvals don't qualify.

146
00:09:21.823 --> 00:09:26.262
So users learn a PIV card, Windows
Hello for Business or a passkey,

147
00:09:26.262 --> 00:09:28.859
with at least two methods registered.

148
00:09:28.859 --> 00:09:34.196
The course has six short modules:
sign-in; reset and recovery; devices;

149
00:09:34.196 --> 00:09:39.723
where files now live; reporting; and
what the help desk will and won't ask.

150
00:09:39.723 --> 00:09:44.259
Password lessons teach length and
passphrases, not character classes.

151
00:09:44.259 --> 00:09:46.091
The last module matters most,

152
00:09:46.091 --> 00:09:50.135
because the help desk becomes a
target the moment users trust it.

153
00:09:50.135 --> 00:09:55.273
CISA's Scattered Spider advisory
describes attackers posing as I T staff.

154
00:09:55.273 --> 00:09:59.998
The real help desk never asks for a
password, a PIN or a code, never asks you to

155
00:09:59.998 --> 00:10:01.625
approve a prompt for them,

156
00:10:01.625 --> 00:10:05.480
and never sends a pass to an
address you give on the call.

157
00:10:06.230 --> 00:10:09.680
Ask who owns training and
three offices answer at once.

158
00:10:09.680 --> 00:10:14.405
Between them, nobody owns whether people
can do the new job on the day it changes.

159
00:10:14.405 --> 00:10:16.905
So the program keeps one decider per row.

160
00:10:16.905 --> 00:10:19.432
The sponsor and C I O fund the learning

161
00:10:19.432 --> 00:10:22.530
program and decide each
wave's readiness plan.

162
00:10:22.530 --> 00:10:26.433
The CISO owns the training
policy, the completion threshold,

163
00:10:26.433 --> 00:10:31.330
and the exact words users are taught about
sign-in, reporting and the help desk.

164
00:10:31.330 --> 00:10:35.386
The chief human capital officer's
office owns workforce strategy,

165
00:10:35.386 --> 00:10:39.443
position coding, the facts behind
the rosters, and labor relations.

166
00:10:39.443 --> 00:10:43.080
The training lead builds the
curriculum and reports the numbers,

167
00:10:43.080 --> 00:10:44.831
but never declares a wave ready.

168
00:10:44.831 --> 00:10:47.755
The I S S O reviews the training record.

169
00:10:47.755 --> 00:10:51.092
The program office puts
training into every gate.

170
00:10:51.092 --> 00:10:55.078
Supervisors own completion;
the help desk manager decides

171
00:10:55.078 --> 00:10:58.981
whether the desk is ready;
communications owns one sender.

172
00:10:58.981 --> 00:11:01.442
Contractors can build and deliver it all,

173
00:11:01.442 --> 00:11:04.560
but government officials
make the decisions.

174
00:11:05.310 --> 00:11:07.910
The curriculum runs on
the roadmap's calendar.

175
00:11:07.910 --> 00:11:12.826
The planning rule is simple: no gate is
scheduled without its training dates, and no

176
00:11:12.826 --> 00:11:17.598
wave without its training window, so a wave
that slips moves its training with it.

177
00:11:17.598 --> 00:11:21.148
Each gate carries a training
check beside its technical exit.

178
00:11:21.148 --> 00:11:24.194
At the foundation gate,
the help desk is trained on

179
00:11:24.194 --> 00:11:27.985
verification and administrators
on just-in-time activation.

180
00:11:27.985 --> 00:11:31.273
Pilot and division users are
trained before their waves.

181
00:11:31.273 --> 00:11:35.759
And by the last gate, no course still
teaches an Active Directory procedure.

182
00:11:35.759 --> 00:11:39.968
The help desk goes first: no
technician is made eligible for a help

183
00:11:39.968 --> 00:11:44.410
desk role until their training and
verification check are on record.

184
00:11:44.410 --> 00:11:49.216
Before each wave, champions and managers
start sixty to forty-five days out,

185
00:11:49.216 --> 00:11:54.486
the help desk is trained by thirty days,
users are assigned their modules at thirty,

186
00:11:54.486 --> 00:11:59.030
with reminders at fifteen and seven,
and then the readiness check.

187
00:11:59.780 --> 00:12:02.517
A completion record says
someone finished a course.

188
00:12:02.517 --> 00:12:06.156
Readiness says they can do the
job on the day the change arrives.

189
00:12:06.156 --> 00:12:11.272
Following NIST's learning guide, the
program measures four levels: reaction,

190
00:12:11.272 --> 00:12:15.251
from surveys; learning, from
assessments and labs; behavior,

191
00:12:15.251 --> 00:12:19.150
from help desk ticket trends,
sign-in method registration

192
00:12:19.150 --> 00:12:22.967
and verification audits; and
results, from gate outcomes.

193
00:12:22.967 --> 00:12:25.382
The rule is completion for everyone,

194
00:12:25.382 --> 00:12:29.728
and competence for every role where
a mistake is a security event:

195
00:12:29.728 --> 00:12:33.591
help desk technicians, privileged
administrators, I S S Os,

196
00:12:33.591 --> 00:12:37.293
and the people named in
incident and contingency plans.

197
00:12:37.293 --> 00:12:41.029
For them, completion without a
passing assessment doesn't count.

198
00:12:41.029 --> 00:12:44.643
Registration by wave is the
most direct end-user measure,

199
00:12:44.643 --> 00:12:46.642
and it costs nothing to collect.

200
00:12:46.642 --> 00:12:49.065
A wave that isn't ready moves its date,

201
00:12:49.065 --> 00:12:52.455
not its threshold, and the
pilot sets the baselines.

202
00:12:52.455 --> 00:12:56.848
The steering committee gets one
page at every meeting, in counts,

203
00:12:56.848 --> 00:13:02.020
not names, with privileged gaps as a
count, because each one is a finding.

204
00:13:02.770 --> 00:13:04.145
So, four decisions.

205
00:13:04.145 --> 00:13:07.283
Put training in every
gate, measured against a

206
00:13:07.283 --> 00:13:10.833
roster from HR and identity
records, not a headcount.

207
00:13:10.833 --> 00:13:15.046
A gate passes with a gap only
as a named, dated exception.

208
00:13:15.046 --> 00:13:17.548
Train and verify the help desk first,

209
00:13:17.548 --> 00:13:21.220
before any new role is assigned
and before every wave.

210
00:13:21.220 --> 00:13:23.499
Name the owners and keep them apart:

211
00:13:23.499 --> 00:13:27.506
the chief human capital officer
and the C I O set competencies,

212
00:13:27.506 --> 00:13:33.007
the CISO owns the security words, and the
training lead reports while others decide.

213
00:13:33.007 --> 00:13:38.433
And certify with artifacts: capstones
and two credentials, renewed every year.

214
00:13:38.433 --> 00:13:40.632
The next step needs no procurement.

215
00:13:40.632 --> 00:13:43.079
Build the role roster from HR records,

216
00:13:43.079 --> 00:13:46.236
coded positions and
eligible-role assignments;

217
00:13:46.236 --> 00:13:51.365
name a training lead and an owner for each
track; and brief the workforce office,

218
00:13:51.365 --> 00:13:56.258
labor relations and the Section 508
program manager before the first gate.

219
00:13:56.258 --> 00:13:59.021
People, not tools, close the gap.

220
00:13:59.021 --> 00:14:02.745
Book 16 has the detail,
every claim footnoted.

221
00:14:02.745 --> 00:14:03.560
Thank you.
